worker_module_budget
  module "formal_ai_worker_web_formalize.js"
  ceiling 1023
  rationale "Issue #1163 generic page formalizer, browser twin (R1163-10, JS-first doctrine). JavaScript twin of rust/src/web_formalize.rs and web_formalize_trust.rs (mime hints and sniffing, the HTML walker with container stepping, comment and opaque-tag skipping and close-tag matching, Markdown, JSON and plain-text blocks, seed-driven language tags, the page store keyed by URL and SHA-256, the two page queries with their seed templates and the trust score), added 2026-10-07; growth is the ported formalizer, not silent regrowth. Ceiling moved 770 to 969 for the issue #1163 drafting batch, the browser twins of the Rust paths: definition terms as list items (R3), the remembered-capture replay (R6), computed trust features with the Wikidata P856 reader and the registry primacy (R7), the page_query_text route over a page the prompt supplies (R10) and the document-conversion source (R13), with the page indexing shared by fetched and supplied pages. Ceiling moved 969 to 990 for the byte-for-byte captures of issues #1163 R14 and #1164 R12, mirroring rust/src/web_formalize.rs: an element carrying a seed html_code_attribute such as the data-lang of kotlinlang.org is a code block in that language, a list item holding block children is stepped into, and a bare pre inherits the language class of its enclosing container as on docs.scala-lang.org. Ceiling moved 990 to 992 for issue #1164 R1164-11: tryPageQueryText first offers the supplied page to tryCodeExamplePageQuery (formal_ai_worker_code_examples.js), as handle_page_query_text does in Rust. Ceiling moved 992 to 1023 for issue #1165 R1165-1: pageRemoveTags is shared by the prose and code strippers, and pageStripTagsKeepLines keeps a code block code (every line keeps its indentation and inner spacing, the blank lines around the block and the indentation all lines share are dropped), mirroring strip_tags_keep_lines in rust/src/web_formalize.rs; the per-line prose stripper had removed the indentation of every program recomposed from a captured page."
